/target/
/release/
/module/bin/

# actions/download-artifact unpacks here in the packaging job. Untracked and
# unignored, it made `git status --porcelain` emit `?? artifacts/`, which the
# +dirty exclusion in package.sh does not filter -- so EVERY CI zip, tagged
# releases included, was stamped as not matching the commit it names. A suffix
# that is always on is a suffix nobody reads. Same failure as /nm-arm64 below.
/artifacts/

# Transient output of the zig `nm` build. package.sh removes its own copy,
# but the CI workflow builds nm in a separate step and leaves this behind --
# which made `git status --porcelain` report `?? nm-arm64` and stamped EVERY
# CI zip, including tagged releases, as `+dirty`. A suffix that is always
# present is a suffix nobody reads.
/nm-arm64

# zig's LOCAL build cache. `zig cc` puts it in the working directory, which for
# the packaging job is the repo root -- so `git status --porcelain` reported
# `?? .zig-cache/` and package.sh stamped EVERY CI zip `+dirty`, tagged releases
# included (v1.3.118 shipped as `710cdcb+dirty`, v1.3.119 as `8fbb35d+dirty`).
# A suffix that is always on is a suffix nobody reads, and it hid for releases
# because the marker never said WHAT was dirty; it does now.
#
# Not reproducible locally, which is why it took a CI run to name: outside
# Actions `zig cc` resolves its cache under ~/.cache/zig and leaves the tree
# clean (measured, zig 0.14.1). mlugg/setup-zig redirects the GLOBAL cache so it
# can save it between runs, and the local cache then materialises here.
# `zig-cache/` is the pre-0.14 spelling, kept so a toolchain downgrade cannot
# quietly bring the problem back.
/.zig-cache/
/zig-cache/
